1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,080
From a $200 pile of plywood to a stack of rare African veneers worth 20 grand.

2
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:12,820
This is where you'll find the various woods used by the construction industry.

3
00:00:13,780 --> 00:00:19,960
Whether it's timber from last week, or 100 years ago, or 50,000 years ago.

4
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:25,960
It's all cut, shaped, and prepared by an army of workers with an arsenal of mighty tools.

5
00:00:25,961 --> 00:00:32,960
Now, The Lumberyard on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:53,360
You can find virtually any shape, size or species of wood here.

7
00:00:53,361 --> 00:00:54,412
the lumber yard.

8
00:00:54,413 --> 00:00:59,080
It's a vast, busy and noisy complex where technology is brought to bear

9
00:00:59,081 --> 00:01:05,920
on stacks and stacks of lumber that will eventually form the structures we live in.

10
00:01:05,921 --> 00:01:15,000
One of the things we're relearning today is that wood has a unique appeal to us as individuals.

11
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:19,095
We want to be surrounded by wood to build our homes and to beautify our homes.

12
00:01:19,096 --> 00:01:19,796
And that's

13
00:01:19,797 --> 00:01:24,960
That's where the lumber yard is the last mile for delivering that source and those values

14
00:01:24,961 --> 00:01:27,380
to both homeowners and builders.

15
00:01:27,381 --> 00:01:30,200
I got a guy calling in on that.

16
00:01:30,201 --> 00:01:32,000
I want to check that out.

17
00:01:32,001 --> 00:01:37,160
In Richmond, California, Channel Lumber is a traditional lumber yard that sells primarily

18
00:01:37,161 --> 00:01:38,920
to the construction industry.

19
00:01:38,921 --> 00:01:45,900
Over the last 50 years, we started with industry, moved to the local contractors, and then moved

20
00:01:45,901 --> 00:01:50,460
to the elite contractors that actually build America.

21
00:01:50,461 --> 00:01:52,780
People that build Skys Creek in San Francisco.

22
00:01:52,781 --> 00:01:55,660
And at one time we had five bridges going here.

23
00:01:55,661 --> 00:01:58,300
We were delivering to all the five bridges.

24
00:01:58,301 --> 00:02:00,980
And the highways, people don't realize,

25
00:02:00,981 --> 00:02:04,260
but they use a lot of timbers for supporting the concrete.

26
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:09,900
It all starts here,

27
00:02:09,901 --> 00:02:12,140
in stands which have taken decades

28
00:02:12,141 --> 00:02:14,000
or even centuries to mature.

29
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:27,900
Logs are cut into coarse planks at the sawmill and delivered in rough form to the lumberyard.

30
00:02:27,901 --> 00:02:34,900
12 by 12 Douglas fir timber forms the foundation of the West Coast lumber industry.

31
00:02:34,901 --> 00:02:40,900
It can be modified at the lumberyard into a vast variety of wood products.

32
00:02:40,901 --> 00:02:44,580
A resaw is used to change the size of the wood to specific dimensions.

33
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:48,860
Behind me is our largest resaw at this facility.

34
00:02:49,380 --> 00:02:54,060
Its main function is to resaw rough timber that came from the sawmill.

35
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:55,820
That's why we call it a resaw.

36
00:02:57,580 --> 00:03:00,560
We'll be running this. We're going to bust it down the middle, okay?

37
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:03,180
We're about to start up our 60-inch resaw.

38
00:03:03,460 --> 00:03:05,940
It has two 60-inch wheels across.

39
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:09,440
One's down below in the pit where you can't see it.

40
00:03:09,441 --> 00:03:11,180
The one up above you can see.

41
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:16,080
This carries an 8-inch wide blade, 30 feet long.

42
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:23,720
We're going to be center-splitting 12 by 12 Douglas fir into two equal pieces of 6 by 12.

43
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:28,580
The timbers move on rollers that bring them up to the operator,

44
00:03:29,720 --> 00:03:33,620
who then feeds them into the 125-horsepower resaw.

45
00:03:33,621 --> 00:03:39,760
Attached to the machine's two wheels is a bandsaw blade.

46
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:45,600
As it's pulled by the wheels, the blade's razor-sharp teeth cut the wood passing through.

47
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:51,440
This resaw is capable of cutting 300 board feet per minute.

48
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:57,900
Just across the way is a planer, which takes the resawed wood to the next level.

49
00:03:57,901 --> 00:04:05,680
What this machine does is takes rough lumber, serves it to a desired thickness and width.

50
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:13,780
It can surface up to an 8 by 16 dimension, which is very large, down to a 2 by 4, let's say.

51
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:19,280
It's capable of running at speeds of 400 feet per minute.

52
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:25,340
Unlike the resaw, the planer feeds the wood automatically.

53
00:04:25,341 --> 00:04:32,820
And instead of a bandsaw blade, it has four heads, each with eight rotating knives that do the cutting.

54
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:37,660
The wood hits the top and bottom heads in sequence.

55
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:40,200
They're running at 100 horsepower.

56
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:48,980
Now it hits opposing side heads, which are 50 to 35 horsepower apiece.

57
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:50,860
That gives you a final width.

58
00:04:50,861 --> 00:05:01,640
Self feeds out, gets transferred on some transfer chains off to an automatic stacking machine.

59
00:05:01,641 --> 00:05:04,100
One man runs this whole operation.

60
00:05:04,101 --> 00:05:06,760
Okay, looks good.

61
00:05:06,761 --> 00:05:10,560
We start out with Ralph Douglas fir, four by eight.

62
00:05:10,561 --> 00:05:14,480
We've surfaced this down to three and a half by seven and a half.

63
00:05:14,481 --> 00:05:15,480
It's now a nice finish.

64
00:05:15,481 --> 00:05:18,480
It's ready to go to the contractor for building purposes.

65
00:05:18,481 --> 00:05:25,700
This lumber yard stocks around 5 million board feet of lumber on its 14 acres.

66
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:31,080
One board foot is equal to a piece of wood that's one foot square and one inch thick.

67
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:38,660
Since this yard mainly supplies very large jobs, you won't find the finer interior woods here,

68
00:05:39,740 --> 00:05:44,240
like mahogany or maple, which are used for furniture, cabinetry, and flooring.

69
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:47,980
This yard is all about construction.

70
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:55,260
In addition to Douglas Fir, Channel stocks a few other species that have specialized

71
00:05:55,261 --> 00:06:01,480
uses, like redwood, which grows primarily in California but also in Southern Oregon.

72
00:06:01,481 --> 00:06:09,740
Redwood is mainly used for decking, exterior applications, build a trellis, an arbor, anything

73
00:06:09,741 --> 00:06:11,300
outside.

74
00:06:11,301 --> 00:06:14,560
Redwood holds up very well to the weather.

75
00:06:14,561 --> 00:06:18,360
So insects won't attack it and it won't rot.

76
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:21,740
And cedar basically has the same principles.

77
00:06:22,440 --> 00:06:24,120
It's a beautiful product to work with.

78
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:31,540
Channel also creates moldings and shaped wood for a variety of purposes, as specified by the client.

79
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:36,560
For this specialized work, they first have to create a pattern.

80
00:06:36,561 --> 00:06:46,020
As you can see, this machine has a grinding wheel, which must be dressed exactly to the same thickness as your tracing pin.

81
00:06:47,180 --> 00:06:52,460
So we have our template, we have our cutter head, we have our grinding wheel.

82
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:06,340
Using the template, the knife is then ground, following the pattern.

83
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:08,700
just like making a key at the hardware store.

84
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:12,340
This results in a perfect profile knife.

85
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:14,300
Let's take a look at our molder.

86
00:07:15,540 --> 00:07:18,760
Here we have a knife that's been ground.

87
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:23,740
Over here is our customer's profile that we are going to reproduce.

88
00:07:24,520 --> 00:07:29,460
It's known as a tongue and groove, used primarily for a roofing.

89
00:07:30,140 --> 00:07:31,160
So let's see it run.

90
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:50,560
The finished product will be delivered to the roofing contractor who ordered it.

91
00:07:54,020 --> 00:07:58,340
This machine, and in fact all the wood processing activities at the lumberyard, kick up a lot

92
00:07:58,341 --> 00:08:01,920
of dust and shavings.

93
00:08:01,921 --> 00:08:05,800
To deal with this, every lumberyard must have a blower system.

94
00:08:05,801 --> 00:08:10,820
High-efficiency fans called cyclones suck shavings and dust through tubes attached to

95
00:08:10,821 --> 00:08:13,820
every machine in the plant.

96
00:08:13,821 --> 00:08:20,260
Once the debris is captured, it's dropped down into a closed system on a truck for disposal.

97
00:08:20,261 --> 00:08:28,100
From here, it'll go with sold as horse bedding, co-gen fuel, and or particle board plants

98
00:08:28,101 --> 00:08:31,840
will buy our product.

99
00:08:31,841 --> 00:08:33,840
Impressive recycling.

100
00:08:33,841 --> 00:08:40,460
But for some lumberyards that buy and sell salvaged or reclaimed wood, recycling is not

101
00:08:40,461 --> 00:08:45,600
a byproduct, it's the central activity.

102
00:08:45,601 --> 00:08:50,720
In a sleepy little corner of Maine, longleaf lumber specializes in wood that's typically

103
00:08:50,721 --> 00:08:54,220
at least 100 years old.

104
00:08:54,221 --> 00:09:00,460
Or in the case of antique heart pine, is virtually extinct because of over-harvesting.

105
00:09:00,461 --> 00:09:06,580
Our antique heart pine, which is the soul of our business, comes from mill buildings that were built during the Industrial Revolution.

106
00:09:07,180 --> 00:09:11,020
They were built with heart pine sawn in the south and shipped up to the north.

107
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:15,520
We also get a lot of material from barns and houses.

108
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:24,080
The reclaimed wood business began in the 1960s, with roots in the anti-establishment and environmental movements.

109
00:09:25,340 --> 00:09:29,640
Supporters took pride in the fact that they weren't cutting down trees to build or renovate their houses.

110
00:09:30,460 --> 00:09:40,700
And as those hippies have grown older and become retiring CEOs of corporations with 5,000 square foot mansions,

111
00:09:40,840 --> 00:09:44,320
they've bought into this whole market of recycled lumber.

112
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:47,420
It ties them back to the earth and to something that's very personal.

113
00:09:48,980 --> 00:09:55,240
While the market for reclaimed wood has certainly grown, it's less than 1% of the total lumber industry.

114
00:09:56,200 --> 00:09:58,080
Still, that's big business.

115
00:10:00,460 --> 00:10:03,960
About 60 companies nationwide are in the reclaimed lumber trade,

116
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,140
recycling around 250 million board feet annually.

117
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:15,360
These lumberyards typically mill and resell lumber at about four times its original cost.

118
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:20,960
Prices of $16 to $20 a board foot are not unusual.

119
00:10:22,940 --> 00:10:27,500
In Longleaf's lumberyard, a recent shipment of heavily distressed wood has risen from history

120
00:10:27,501 --> 00:10:29,840
and now awaits millwork.

121
00:10:30,460 --> 00:10:35,920
These are white oak pilings that came out of Boston Harbor, Battery Wharf in Boston Harbor.

122
00:10:36,340 --> 00:10:39,240
We believe that they're about 100 years old, but we're not certain.

123
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,160
Some of them are very distressed, as you can see.

124
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:46,080
Others are very solid, but we'll saw these up and make flooring out of these.

125
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:49,920
A lot of them have a lot of very, very deep color,

126
00:10:51,140 --> 00:10:56,080
especially staining from iron that's been in these things for many years.

127
00:10:56,081 --> 00:11:00,960
that's reacted with the salt water and a black-blue kind of stain shoots through the grain

128
00:11:00,961 --> 00:11:06,000
and colors it in a very unusual way, and some people really, really like it.

129
00:11:08,020 --> 00:11:11,200
But typically, the reclaimed wood comes from buildings.

130
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:17,140
Our material comes in in beam form off a demolition site or a dismantling site,

131
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:21,520
and as you can see behind you, it's stacked up in an orderly fashion

132
00:11:21,521 --> 00:11:25,060
so that we can determine what we can yield out of the material.

133
00:11:26,080 --> 00:11:27,947
We pull the metal out of it.

134
00:11:27,948 --> 00:11:33,080
We're using airport wand type metal detectors, personal type metal detectors.

135
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:46,080
And then we saw the material into boards with a band mill.

136
00:11:46,081 --> 00:11:54,240
And we dry the material.

137
00:11:54,241 --> 00:11:58,960
Even though it's quite old and relatively dry, it still isn't necessarily dry enough

138
00:11:58,961 --> 00:12:01,720
for millwork, which is what we do.

139
00:12:01,721 --> 00:12:06,640
Reclaimed wood is dried in a kiln that has high-speed blowers, sending in heated air

140
00:12:06,641 --> 00:12:10,640
for three to four days.

141
00:12:10,641 --> 00:12:18,140
And then we run it through a four-side molder, at which point it is a finished product.

142
00:12:18,141 --> 00:12:28,140
Longleaf's Lumberyard and Mill are in Maine, but its showroom is here in Cambridge, Massachusetts,

143
00:12:28,141 --> 00:12:30,960
where the demand for reclaimed wood is great.

144
00:12:30,961 --> 00:12:36,060
We wanted customers to be able to come in and walk on the different floors that we have

145
00:12:36,061 --> 00:12:37,420
available.

146
00:12:37,421 --> 00:12:43,180
When you see a larger expanse of these installed floors, they get a real sense of what they

147
00:12:43,181 --> 00:12:45,740
can have in their homes.

148
00:12:45,741 --> 00:12:47,620
People aesthetically relate to this.

149
00:12:47,621 --> 00:12:53,460
It's incredibly beautiful, tight-grained, warm tones and colors that you just don't

150
00:12:53,461 --> 00:12:55,460
find in new wood.

151
00:12:55,461 --> 00:13:00,620
This is an example of our select grade heart pine.

152
00:13:00,621 --> 00:13:07,040
In this grade, you will have knots, nail holes, and some mineral staining.

153
00:13:07,041 --> 00:13:11,280
Over here we have our clear heart pine.

154
00:13:11,281 --> 00:13:17,320
Customers if they're looking for a more formal look they'll say I'd like the material without

155
00:13:17,321 --> 00:13:20,880
knots, nail holes, mineral staining.

156
00:13:20,881 --> 00:13:24,420
Chestnut is one of the most valued floors that we have.

157
00:13:24,421 --> 00:13:30,200
The chestnut tree at one time made up 25% of the forest on the eastern seaboard.

158
00:13:30,201 --> 00:13:34,320
After a blight hit it in the early 1900s the trees were decimated.

159
00:13:34,321 --> 00:13:41,320
The only way to have a chestnut floor or millwork is through the salvage process.

160
00:13:41,321 --> 00:13:49,280
In many instances, the rougher, more distressed the condition of the wood, the better.

161
00:13:49,281 --> 00:13:54,400
We surfaced one side, but left the live edge on the outer edge.

162
00:13:54,401 --> 00:14:01,420
Customers love to have this as a countertop or bar top or a mantle.

163
00:14:01,421 --> 00:14:03,680
It shows the original shape of the tree.

164
00:14:04,280 --> 00:14:06,880
This is what they call virgin growth lumber.

165
00:14:09,220 --> 00:14:14,560
Virgin growth lumber comes from trees that were here when European settlers first came to America.

166
00:14:15,660 --> 00:14:22,220
Shortly after the pilgrims landed, they would use that virgin lumber to establish America's earliest lumber yards.

167
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:29,920
8,000 years ago, nearly 15 billion acres of forest covered the earth.

168
00:14:29,921 --> 00:14:34,160
Today, forests cover only about 8 and a half billion acres.

169
00:14:34,161 --> 00:14:41,880
The Lumberyard will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

170
00:14:52,620 --> 00:14:58,800
Here in Boston Harbor nearly 400 years ago, shortly after the Pilgrims landed in America,

171
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:02,220
lumber yards began springing up along the shoreline.

172
00:15:03,400 --> 00:15:06,380
They provided shipbuilders and homesteaders with the wood they needed.

173
00:15:07,520 --> 00:15:11,240
Settlers were especially interested in New England's vast stands of white pine,

174
00:15:11,960 --> 00:15:14,360
readily available to meet the demands of a new society.

175
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:20,560
By the mid-1600s, the Massachusetts Bay colonists had moved into the countryside,

176
00:15:21,820 --> 00:15:24,040
establishing the New England town of Amherst.

177
00:15:24,041 --> 00:15:32,740
It's here that the Coles family founded a commercial lumber yard in 1741.

178
00:15:32,741 --> 00:15:39,860
These were English Puritans who had come to the Boston area with the 1630 migration

179
00:15:39,861 --> 00:15:40,920
from England.

180
00:15:40,921 --> 00:15:44,960
They were into dairy farming, crop farming.

181
00:15:44,961 --> 00:15:48,980
They were also, if they had woods, were selling the lumber.

182
00:15:48,981 --> 00:15:51,380
show that the Coles is very early.

183
00:15:51,381 --> 00:15:56,960
We're selling lumber to other people in the town.

184
00:15:56,961 --> 00:15:59,480
Wood cutting technology was primitive.

185
00:15:59,481 --> 00:16:02,660
The Coles surely used a pit saw.

186
00:16:02,661 --> 00:16:05,980
One man is actually in a hole in the ground.

187
00:16:05,981 --> 00:16:10,400
The log is suspended on a sawhorse sort of thing on top.

188
00:16:10,401 --> 00:16:13,820
And there's another man that stands up above the log.

189
00:16:13,821 --> 00:16:17,500
And they push and pull on the same saw and cut it that way.

190
00:16:18,980 --> 00:16:28,660
They also used a two-handled saw, as demonstrated by Paul Jones and his daughter, Cinda, who

191
00:16:28,661 --> 00:16:31,900
represent the eighth and ninth generations in the family business.

192
00:16:34,660 --> 00:16:38,340
The first lumberyards in the Massachusetts colony were rudimentary.

193
00:16:39,640 --> 00:16:46,120
The earliest lumberyard here would have been probably part of the cow barn, even, or shed

194
00:16:46,121 --> 00:16:52,100
off the cow barn with just stacks of lumber in it that he had accumulated, perhaps some logs or

195
00:16:52,101 --> 00:16:55,270
fence posts or whatever it is he was selling.

196
00:16:55,271 --> 00:16:58,440
Since green or freshly cut wood is subject to

197
00:16:58,441 --> 00:17:04,489
warping, colonists cured the wood by exposing it to the sun for a year or two.

198
00:17:04,490 --> 00:17:06,040
Lumber yards usually

199
00:17:06,041 --> 00:17:09,487
profited as towns grew, and Kohl's was no exception.

200
00:17:09,488 --> 00:17:11,940
The yard provided wood for the town's

201
00:17:11,941 --> 00:17:12,896
expansion.

202
00:17:12,897 --> 00:17:20,540
It supplied wood for Amherst College, the University of Massachusetts, and Emily

203
00:17:20,541 --> 00:17:22,103
Dickinson's family.

204
00:17:22,104 --> 00:17:27,860
When America's railways were constructed in the 19th century, builders

205
00:17:27,861 --> 00:17:32,002
turned to lumberyards for enormous supplies of railroad ties.

206
00:17:32,003 --> 00:17:33,700
Coles profited handsomely

207
00:17:33,701 --> 00:17:35,066
during this period.

208
00:17:35,067 --> 00:17:40,100
The forests that the Coles family owned still provide wood to the mill

209
00:17:40,101 --> 00:17:43,100
and to the lumberyard, which continues to flourish.

210
00:17:45,680 --> 00:17:48,000
We have hardwoods and softwoods.

211
00:17:48,380 --> 00:17:50,320
Softwoods would be pine and hemlock.

212
00:17:51,020 --> 00:17:55,140
Hardwoods would be the oaks and the birches and the maples.

213
00:17:55,900 --> 00:18:00,540
We primarily saw pine, oak, and hemlock here at the sawmill

214
00:18:00,541 --> 00:18:04,460
and saw our other logs to other mills and other users.

215
00:18:04,461 --> 00:18:14,020
This lumber yard has evolved and expanded like many lumber yards, now selling hardware

216
00:18:14,021 --> 00:18:15,500
and other building necessities.

217
00:18:15,501 --> 00:18:21,900
Nine generations ago, my ancestor Jonathan Coles could never have envisioned the enterprise

218
00:18:21,901 --> 00:18:28,280
this has grown to, encompassing 16 acres, almost 50 employees now, a business that you

219
00:18:28,281 --> 00:18:35,700
can build a house with containing lumber, plywood, paint, kitchen cabinets, anything

220
00:18:35,701 --> 00:18:40,440
else you'd want to build your dream home.

221
00:18:40,441 --> 00:18:44,800
The Coles Lumberyard isn't just a part of a family tradition. It's the continuation

222
00:18:44,801 --> 00:18:48,988
of a human enterprise that dates back thousands of years.

223
00:18:48,989 --> 00:18:52,000
Wherever a coastal seafaring civilization

224
00:18:52,001 --> 00:18:54,147
took root, there was a lumberyard.

225
00:18:54,148 --> 00:18:57,620
All ships were made of wood, and someone had to provide

226
00:18:57,621 --> 00:19:06,352
the lumber. One of the first recorded lumberyard transactions dates back to 1200 BC.

227
00:19:06,353 --> 00:19:07,600
A Phoenician

228
00:19:07,601 --> 00:19:12,272
prince had a well-stocked yard on the Mediterranean shore in what is now Lebanon.

229
00:19:12,273 --> 00:19:13,080
These were the

230
00:19:13,081 --> 00:19:18,140
famous cedars of Lebanon, huge trees that were equivalent to anything you would find

231
00:19:18,141 --> 00:19:20,069
in the Pacific Northwest.

232
00:19:20,070 --> 00:19:24,620
And these trees went to build the ships of Egypt, the ships

233
00:19:24,621 --> 00:19:27,325
of Phoenicia and the ships of Israel.

234
00:19:27,326 --> 00:19:31,420
Any power in that part of the world required a Phoenicia

235
00:19:31,421 --> 00:19:32,540
for their ships.

236
00:19:32,541 --> 00:19:38,000
And because dominance at sea contributed to a kingdom's strength and security,

237
00:19:38,700 --> 00:19:41,380
whoever ran a lumberyard wielded power as well.

238
00:19:44,080 --> 00:19:49,480
Around 1000 B.C., a large quantity of lumber was brokered in connection with one of the

239
00:19:49,481 --> 00:19:51,040
most famous temples in history.

240
00:19:52,600 --> 00:19:54,640
A Phoenician prince named Sirius,

241
00:19:54,820 --> 00:19:58,580
who had a lumberyard on the eastern coast of the Mediterranean.

242
00:19:59,020 --> 00:20:00,780
And from there, he provided King Solomon

243
00:20:00,781 --> 00:20:02,680
with gigantic quantities of cedar

244
00:20:02,681 --> 00:20:05,260
that was used to frame the temple built in Jerusalem.

245
00:20:06,040 --> 00:20:08,300
The lumber trade expanded exponentially

246
00:20:08,301 --> 00:20:10,300
in 12th and 13th century Europe,

247
00:20:11,040 --> 00:20:13,200
especially in Germany, England, and Italy,

248
00:20:13,780 --> 00:20:15,220
when cities began to grow.

249
00:20:16,960 --> 00:20:18,540
Unlike in ancient times,

250
00:20:18,541 --> 00:20:25,240
When they built these cities with brick and mortar or with stone, these new city-states were built with large quantities of lumber.

251
00:20:26,720 --> 00:20:30,460
Lumberyards appeared in major port cities because that's where the shipyards were.

252
00:20:31,300 --> 00:20:35,740
The Venice Arsenal, a naval depot, held what was perhaps Europe's biggest lumberyard.

253
00:20:36,400 --> 00:20:42,900
The Arsenal was heavily guarded because if they lost their timber supply, they lost their power.

254
00:20:42,901 --> 00:20:53,480
When Europeans came to the American Northeast, their first major industries were shipbuilding and sawmills, which provided the necessary pine lumber.

255
00:20:55,000 --> 00:21:03,395
In some cases, the sawmills had lumber yards adjacent to them, but they also ended up setting up separate lumber yards because of the popularity and the need for

256
00:21:03,396 --> 00:21:04,096
lumber.

257
00:21:05,460 --> 00:21:08,620
America's westward expansion brought further growth to the lumber trade.

258
00:21:08,621 --> 00:21:15,620
First, the settlers built their homes by cutting trees themselves.

259
00:21:15,621 --> 00:21:21,620
As these initial settled areas were cut over and as towns began to develop,

260
00:21:21,621 --> 00:21:24,620
then there was a need for shipping in lumber for more distant points,

261
00:21:24,621 --> 00:21:28,620
and then there was a demand for a central type of location

262
00:21:28,621 --> 00:21:34,620
where lumber could be purchased by local homeowners and contractors and businesses.

263
00:21:34,621 --> 00:21:43,120
businesses. When the railroad arrived in 1861, Chicago benefited from its central

264
00:21:43,121 --> 00:21:47,940
location as well as its proximity via the Chicago River to the lumber stands of

265
00:21:47,941 --> 00:21:53,120
the Great Lakes region. It became the lumberyard capital of the Midwest with

266
00:21:53,121 --> 00:21:59,040
an extensive lumber district. The railroad had a big impact on the spread of lumber

267
00:21:59,041 --> 00:22:03,060
yards because every town that was a stop on the railroad would have a yard

268
00:22:03,061 --> 00:22:06,460
because the lumber would be shipped in, would sit in that yard,

269
00:22:06,580 --> 00:22:10,480
and then be distributed out to the residents of the little towns as they built up.

270
00:22:11,500 --> 00:22:16,160
The mid-19th century saw a major technological change in the lumberyard.

271
00:22:17,740 --> 00:22:22,220
The bandsaw helped lumberyards expand from being mere warehouses

272
00:22:22,221 --> 00:22:25,300
to businesses that could cut wood to order.

273
00:22:27,540 --> 00:22:32,500
Around that same time, the gold rush lured people to the West Coast and San Francisco.

274
00:22:33,060 --> 00:22:36,400
There was a tremendous explosion in the growth of the city,

275
00:22:36,840 --> 00:22:39,660
homes needing to be built and businesses and so on.

276
00:22:40,100 --> 00:22:44,560
And shipbuilding was one of the first industries that was taken on when gold was discovered.

277
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:49,200
So lumberyards became an intrinsic part of the skyline of early San Francisco.

278
00:22:50,400 --> 00:22:56,160
Lumber came from California, the Pacific Northwest, and even as far away as New England.

279
00:22:57,800 --> 00:23:00,980
The Northeast also supplied many lumberyard entrepreneurs.

280
00:23:00,981 --> 00:23:05,500
men with lumber backgrounds who'd come west to seize a new opportunity.

281
00:23:07,380 --> 00:23:11,720
These folks quickly realized that there was a limited amount of money that could be made in the gold fields,

282
00:23:11,960 --> 00:23:15,320
but there was almost an unlimited amount that could be made in the lumber industry.

283
00:23:16,340 --> 00:23:21,020
As a result, the San Francisco Bay Area became the lumberyard capital of the West.

284
00:23:23,040 --> 00:23:27,000
Lumber dealers flourished with San Francisco's boom and tragedies.

285
00:23:27,001 --> 00:23:35,000
Remarkably, from 1849 to 1851, San Francisco suffered six devastating fires.

286
00:23:35,001 --> 00:23:46,000
Then in 1906, it was struck by a massive earthquake and subsequent fire.

287
00:23:46,001 --> 00:23:52,000
The Bay Area's busy lumber yard slowed down during wartime and the Depression.

288
00:23:52,001 --> 00:23:59,000
But after World War II, lumber was again in great demand in San Francisco and across America.

289
00:23:59,001 --> 00:24:06,000
Returning GIs could easily qualify for home loans, and the construction business grew spectacularly.

290
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:15,000
Today, with a nationwide building boom, lumber yards continue to flourish.

291
00:24:15,001 --> 00:24:21,000
But in addition to traditional lumber, the yards now provide products that have revolutionized the construction industry.

292
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:27,000
engineered woods

293
00:24:27,001 --> 00:24:32,440
US lumber manufacturers shipped nearly 39 billion board feet of softwood lumber

294
00:24:32,441 --> 00:24:39,580
in 2004 that's enough to build a bridge more than 30 feet wide from earth to the

295
00:24:39,581 --> 00:24:48,460
moon the lumberyard will return on modern marbles in the construction

296
00:24:48,461 --> 00:24:53,500
industry, builders have always looked for ways to do things faster and cheaper, while

297
00:24:53,501 --> 00:24:56,420
still maintaining quality.

298
00:24:56,421 --> 00:25:04,320
So, for much of the 20th century, they went to the lumber yard to purchase plywood.

299
00:25:04,321 --> 00:25:08,520
Large sturdy sheets of engineered wood.

300
00:25:08,521 --> 00:25:16,400
It's used for framing, roofing, and flooring.

301
00:25:16,401 --> 00:25:21,000
eliminates the need for hammering together many smaller pieces of wood, which is the

302
00:25:21,001 --> 00:25:24,400
way homes were constructed before plywood came along.

303
00:25:24,401 --> 00:25:32,980
So we're able to develop a stronger, lighter, easier to install, faster to install home

304
00:25:32,981 --> 00:25:38,920
than is typically possible with solid sawn lumber.

305
00:25:38,921 --> 00:25:43,340
Plywood actually dates back to the Egyptians.

306
00:25:43,341 --> 00:25:48,360
The oldest example of plywood was found inside a third dynasty coffin made of six layers

307
00:25:48,361 --> 00:25:52,880
of wood, each four millimeters thick and held together by wooden pegs.

308
00:25:52,881 --> 00:25:59,860
Plywood was first patented in New York City in 1865, but it was perfected in the early

309
00:25:59,861 --> 00:26:04,700
20th century with better glues bonding the sheets under heat.

310
00:26:04,701 --> 00:26:12,400
Plywood's big advantages include its standard size of four feet by eight feet, its low cost,

311
00:26:12,401 --> 00:26:15,860
its strength and stability.

312
00:26:15,861 --> 00:26:18,840
To make plywood, a log is softened with steam

313
00:26:18,841 --> 00:26:20,040
and then mounted on a lathe.

314
00:26:23,520 --> 00:26:26,540
A long, sharp knife peels off a thin layer of wood,

315
00:26:26,541 --> 00:26:27,860
which is called a veneer.

316
00:26:34,800 --> 00:26:38,900
Sections called plies are cut and then set aside to dry.

317
00:26:42,400 --> 00:26:49,880
The next step is the gluing of the plies together using synthetic plastics such as urea resins or phenol formaldehyde.

318
00:26:52,080 --> 00:26:57,360
Several sheets of veneer are layered together with the grain direction alternating between the different layers.

319
00:26:58,720 --> 00:27:08,220
And what happens there is that you actually have the strength enhanced by this cross pattern of the grain.

320
00:27:08,221 --> 00:27:14,640
Otherwise, a single sheet of wood this thin might crack along a weak spot in the grain.

321
00:27:17,220 --> 00:27:21,040
The stack is then heated to around 250 degrees Fahrenheit

322
00:27:21,041 --> 00:27:26,180
and squeezed under great pressure by hydraulic presses to form a rigid panel.

323
00:27:27,720 --> 00:27:29,360
The glue dries in minutes.

324
00:27:32,140 --> 00:27:34,740
Finally, the plywood panels are trimmed and sanded.

325
00:27:35,360 --> 00:27:37,700
They're now ready for shipment to the lumberyard.

326
00:27:38,220 --> 00:27:44,260
But in recent years, plywood's position as the most popular engineered wood of the construction

327
00:27:44,261 --> 00:27:48,040
industry has been overtaken by another product.

328
00:27:48,041 --> 00:27:53,540
The traditional veneer plywood that most of us are familiar with has recently been passed

329
00:27:53,541 --> 00:28:00,220
by oriented strand board, or what looks like flakes of material, which use smaller diameter

330
00:28:00,221 --> 00:28:05,760
species and faster growing species and yet have the same strength and durability properties

331
00:28:05,761 --> 00:28:06,760
as veneer plywood.

332
00:28:08,220 --> 00:28:14,660
Drive by any construction project, and chances are you'll see oriented strand board, or OSB.

333
00:28:17,280 --> 00:28:23,340
It contains layers of flaked wood in specific crisscrossed orientations that are as strong as plywood.

334
00:28:25,400 --> 00:28:28,160
OSB's chief advantage over plywood is that it's cheaper.

335
00:28:28,161 --> 00:28:38,480
What they're doing is taking wood that ordinarily, or let's say in the old days, may have just gone out in a pile out back because they didn't know what to do with it.

336
00:28:40,180 --> 00:28:48,640
The process begins with logs being fed into a machine that shreds them into wafer-thin strands up to six inches long and one inch wide.

337
00:28:48,641 --> 00:28:55,300
After the strands have been mixed with wax and resin binders for eventual bonding, they're

338
00:28:55,301 --> 00:29:00,140
sifted and arranged into approximately parallel lines.

339
00:29:00,141 --> 00:29:07,620
Next, the loosely adhering material is cut into mats.

340
00:29:07,621 --> 00:29:12,300
The mats are then stacked so that the strands of each one, like plywood, are at opposing

341
00:29:12,301 --> 00:29:14,160
angles to each other.

342
00:29:14,161 --> 00:29:17,680
This creates OSB's strength.

343
00:29:17,681 --> 00:29:21,280
The outer layers of the stack contain randomly oriented mats.

344
00:29:21,281 --> 00:29:27,960
When the stack is subjected to intense heat and pressure, the bonding resins are activated

345
00:29:27,961 --> 00:29:32,000
and a master panel is formed.

346
00:29:32,001 --> 00:29:39,160
In the final step, these panels are cut to size, typically 4 by 8.

347
00:29:39,161 --> 00:29:43,720
Besides oriented strand board and plywood, there are other engineered wood products that

348
00:29:43,721 --> 00:29:47,460
combine wood with high strength adhesives.

349
00:29:47,461 --> 00:29:50,800
glue lamp is one such product.

350
00:29:50,801 --> 00:29:57,180
You can't buy a single piece of wood to 100 feet long.

351
00:29:57,181 --> 00:30:02,240
To get beams of unusually long lengths for large construction projects, smaller pieces

352
00:30:02,241 --> 00:30:06,180
of wood are glued together in the glue lamp process.

353
00:30:06,181 --> 00:30:07,580
The first step?

354
00:30:07,581 --> 00:30:14,440
Wood is kiln dried at the factory and then planed to smoothness and the correct dimensions.

355
00:30:14,441 --> 00:30:20,820
And it goes to the finger joint line where we apply a structural finger joint to each

356
00:30:20,821 --> 00:30:23,200
end of the laminations.

357
00:30:23,201 --> 00:30:26,900
And our structural glue is applied to the finger joint.

358
00:30:26,901 --> 00:30:34,680
And then those pieces now run in line to a mating line which then travels through a

359
00:30:34,681 --> 00:30:38,060
radial frequency tunnel.

360
00:30:38,061 --> 00:30:41,860
The RF radial frequency tunnel is like a microwave oven.

361
00:30:41,861 --> 00:30:45,400
accelerates the cure bonding of the finger joint glue.

362
00:30:47,120 --> 00:30:51,840
The laminated sections are now cut to required length for the beam that's being manufactured.

363
00:30:54,480 --> 00:30:57,300
Glue is then applied to the wide face of the laminates.

364
00:30:59,800 --> 00:31:07,240
The laminations run down to the glue press where they are set and then are pressed together

365
00:31:07,241 --> 00:31:13,840
with these glue blocks to create the structural bonding that's necessary for the glue laminated beam.

366
00:31:17,480 --> 00:31:21,360
Next, a worker pounds the glue lamps to align them before the glue sets.

367
00:31:24,420 --> 00:31:30,540
Another worker uses a pneumatic gun to screw in rods that will hold the glued laminations together with great pressure.

368
00:31:31,980 --> 00:31:33,800
Drying takes six to eight hours.

369
00:31:33,801 --> 00:31:40,800
The planer now is going to plane both sides at the same time into a final net dimension.

370
00:31:43,800 --> 00:31:47,800
In the finish room, the various glue lambs are sanded and cleaned up.

371
00:31:47,801 --> 00:31:51,800
Glue lambs are crafted into many different forms.

372
00:31:51,801 --> 00:31:54,800
One is a glue laminated beam truss.

373
00:31:54,801 --> 00:31:56,800
You can see the connector plates.

374
00:31:56,801 --> 00:32:01,800
And these are factory assembled and delivered to the job site, ready to install.

375
00:32:03,800 --> 00:32:10,900
As useful as these man-made products are, they rarely turn up at lumberyards dealing in exotic and specialty woods.

376
00:32:11,860 --> 00:32:15,200
There, it's the exquisite handiwork of nature that rules.

377
00:32:19,600 --> 00:32:27,260
Of all species of woods, none seem to fuel the imaginations of homeowners and designers, like exotic woods.

378
00:32:28,160 --> 00:32:33,720
These are rare species with unique hues and grain patterns, which don't occur in domestic woods.

379
00:32:33,800 --> 00:32:41,120
They come from places like South America, Africa, and Asia, and are sold in specialty

380
00:32:41,121 --> 00:32:42,120
lumberyards.

381
00:32:42,121 --> 00:32:47,560
We're exposed to many more cultures and experiences than people were 50 or 100 years

382
00:32:47,561 --> 00:32:48,560
ago.

383
00:32:48,561 --> 00:32:53,820
And the appeal of exotic wood is it allows a much wider range of beauty to come into

384
00:32:53,821 --> 00:32:56,320
people's homes.

385
00:32:56,321 --> 00:33:06,220
Whether it's wood for cabinetry, a musical instrument, a yacht interior, or even a million

386
00:33:06,221 --> 00:33:13,520
dollar bar in a five star hotel, want wins out over need when it comes to exotic woods.

387
00:33:13,521 --> 00:33:17,760
For centuries, only royals could afford such beauty and luxury.

388
00:33:17,761 --> 00:33:24,600
But when the industrial revolution created a middle class, it also created consumers

389
00:33:24,601 --> 00:33:28,920
of imported woods like mahogany and teak.

390
00:33:28,921 --> 00:33:33,480
Today like the royalty of a bygone era, the upper middle classes also want to surround

391
00:33:33,481 --> 00:33:34,480
themselves with beauty.

392
00:33:34,481 --> 00:33:38,720
And one of the ways they do so is with exotic woods.

393
00:33:38,721 --> 00:33:45,540
They've got the money to pay for a premium or unique or exotic material that has a unique

394
00:33:45,541 --> 00:33:50,260
appeal or look.

395
00:33:50,261 --> 00:33:56,880
From South America come well-known species like rosewood and mahogany.

396
00:33:56,881 --> 00:34:04,660
Even more exotic are bloodwood, purpleheart, and cocobolo.

397
00:34:04,661 --> 00:34:11,120
From Asia there's camphorwood and ebony macassar.

398
00:34:11,121 --> 00:34:17,160
From Africa come the beautiful colors and grains of zebra wood and bakote.

399
00:34:17,161 --> 00:34:26,080
Twenty years ago, in the Puget Sound village of Portsmouth, Washington, two partners from

400
00:34:26,081 --> 00:34:32,280
the village's wooden boat building community saw a need and filled it with a company called

401
00:34:32,281 --> 00:34:35,100
Edensaw Wood.

402
00:34:35,101 --> 00:34:40,380
We realized that there was a larger market probably for the wood than our skills as shipwrights.

403
00:34:40,381 --> 00:34:43,980
And so we started Edensaw on that basis.

404
00:34:43,981 --> 00:34:48,560
Today, Edensaw is a multi-million dollar business.

405
00:34:48,561 --> 00:34:51,700
Its Portsmouth facility is situated on seven acres

406
00:34:51,701 --> 00:34:54,560
and is continually expanding.

407
00:34:54,561 --> 00:34:58,880
It offers over 60 species of fine and exotic wood.

408
00:34:58,881 --> 00:35:00,940
We've supplied wood for Bill Gates' house

409
00:35:00,941 --> 00:35:02,920
for some of Paul Allen's residences.

410
00:35:02,921 --> 00:35:05,460
We've supplied material for Neil Young's boat

411
00:35:05,461 --> 00:35:09,620
and Steve Miller's boat and others of that type.

412
00:35:10,380 --> 00:35:12,100
Just how much are these woods worth?

413
00:35:12,680 --> 00:35:14,420
Take African Padauk, for example.

414
00:35:15,800 --> 00:35:18,920
Ordinarily pricey, it can be exorbitant,

415
00:35:19,200 --> 00:35:21,580
especially when unique patterns appear in a log.

416
00:35:22,020 --> 00:35:24,000
The log had a very special figure to it,

417
00:35:24,040 --> 00:35:25,680
which made it worth slicing into veneers.

418
00:35:25,681 --> 00:35:30,200
It had a bird's eye effect, very small, little eye-looking figures,

419
00:35:30,320 --> 00:35:32,980
all packed in next to each other, just covering the whole thing.

420
00:35:33,420 --> 00:35:36,960
That log sliced into veneer, sold for in excess of $100,000.

421
00:35:37,400 --> 00:35:39,020
It was an amazing thing.

422
00:35:40,380 --> 00:35:49,940
Edensault began its business by supplying the yachting industry, and that sector is still of primary importance, accounting for 40% of the firm's business.

423
00:35:52,100 --> 00:35:56,280
As with Padauk, many of the veneers used in yachts come from Africa.

424
00:35:57,360 --> 00:36:02,400
This particular exotic veneer is anagre. It's a Central African species of wood.

425
00:36:02,401 --> 00:36:12,137
This particular log is very figured, very curly, and if you look closely you can see that it's about a 42nd of an inch thick, or .6 millimeter, which in inches is

426
00:36:12,138 --> 00:36:14,060
about 23 thousandths of an inch.

427
00:36:14,720 --> 00:36:24,316
This particular pile of material here is in the neighborhood of about 11,000 square feet at the moment, and it's worth about 20,000 U.S.

428
00:36:24,317 --> 00:36:25,017
dollars.

429
00:36:27,280 --> 00:36:29,620
But it's not just veneers that are in demand.

430
00:36:30,040 --> 00:36:31,800
Sometimes solid pieces are called for.

431
00:36:32,400 --> 00:36:36,840
This log is about 53 inches wide.

432
00:36:37,780 --> 00:36:39,660
Bubinga is also known as African rosewood.

433
00:36:40,260 --> 00:36:42,960
And what the craftspeople will do with this is they'll take it,

434
00:36:43,480 --> 00:36:49,880
they'll finish it down to a final rough piece of wood,

435
00:36:49,881 --> 00:36:53,180
and then they'll put whatever finish the owner wants on it.

436
00:36:53,220 --> 00:36:56,920
This will end up as probably a corporate conference table.

437
00:36:57,540 --> 00:37:00,160
And this piece of wood is worth about $2,000 as it sits.

438
00:37:00,161 --> 00:37:05,480
So it's quite nice material, and it's somewhat rare in the United States.

439
00:37:08,880 --> 00:37:12,320
When woods are rare, dealers must walk a fine line.

440
00:37:12,900 --> 00:37:16,360
They can't be too rare, because then they could be classified as endangered.

441
00:37:17,260 --> 00:37:20,460
For many years, Brazilian rosewood was very popular,

442
00:37:21,060 --> 00:37:23,840
especially among guitar makers who used it for fretboards.

443
00:37:24,720 --> 00:37:27,680
But rosewood's popularity caused it to become endangered,

444
00:37:27,681 --> 00:37:30,600
and instrument makers stopped buying it from Brazil.

445
00:37:31,060 --> 00:37:35,340
Brazilian rosewood is one of the favorite species from that territory

446
00:37:35,341 --> 00:37:38,020
that has been over-harvested for a long time.

447
00:37:38,540 --> 00:37:40,520
It's one of the woods we do not have,

448
00:37:40,700 --> 00:37:42,980
and I would not suppose that we could ever get any.

449
00:37:44,100 --> 00:37:46,660
So for now, rosewood comes from other nations.

450
00:37:47,680 --> 00:37:50,340
Some of the rosewoods and some of the ebony and so on

451
00:37:50,341 --> 00:37:53,360
are still brought in from different parts of the world

452
00:37:53,361 --> 00:37:54,920
that still allow the export of it

453
00:37:54,921 --> 00:37:57,800
and that the United States still allows the import from.

454
00:37:59,160 --> 00:38:03,520
For the immediate future, exotic woods will continue to enter the U.S.,

455
00:38:04,160 --> 00:38:07,240
and lumberyards carrying them will continue to flourish.

456
00:38:10,560 --> 00:38:13,440
Some exotic woods don't just come from distant places.

457
00:38:14,340 --> 00:38:16,240
They can come from distant times.

458
00:38:16,980 --> 00:38:20,680
Believe it or not, some are over 50,000 years old.

459
00:38:20,681 --> 00:38:31,160
Woods that are ideal for making musical instruments are known as tonewoods because of their unique ability to conduct sound vibrations.

460
00:38:32,160 --> 00:38:38,280
The Lumberyard will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

461
00:38:44,100 --> 00:38:50,340
There are lumberyards that deal with greenwood, wood that has only recently been harvested from the forest.

462
00:38:50,680 --> 00:38:55,680
Others deal with recycled wood, wood that has been used previously.

463
00:38:55,681 --> 00:39:03,680
But there are some lumber yards that deal exclusively with wood that, like gold or diamonds, is literally mined.

464
00:39:03,681 --> 00:39:11,680
The Great Lakes hold a treasure trove of American and Canadian woods from a bygone era.

465
00:39:11,681 --> 00:39:19,680
In the days of logging, the sawmills were typically located on rivers flowing into or out of the Great Lakes.

466
00:39:19,681 --> 00:39:28,620
So as the logging operations moved, they had a need to transport logs, generally in large rafts, across the Great Lakes.

467
00:39:30,060 --> 00:39:36,920
But when accidents occurred, in storms, rough waters, or collisions, logs broke loose.

468
00:39:38,760 --> 00:39:41,260
Logs that became waterlogged sank.

469
00:39:43,140 --> 00:39:47,260
The cold water refrigerated the wood, preserving it for more than a century.

470
00:39:47,261 --> 00:39:53,580
These logs, called sinkers, not only survived, they also maintained their beauty.

471
00:39:53,581 --> 00:39:58,300
They've come from, in some cases, very old growth forests,

472
00:39:58,301 --> 00:40:02,380
which grew slowly over time and have some very fine ring structures.

473
00:40:02,381 --> 00:40:08,000
Some of the colors that happened because of its life underneath the waters

474
00:40:08,001 --> 00:40:13,300
are really subtly different than a modern harvested tree

475
00:40:13,301 --> 00:40:18,560
that has gone from the log to the mill to the kiln.

476
00:40:24,840 --> 00:40:29,980
Sonar is used in the recovery process to find sinkers between 8 and 40 feet long.

477
00:40:32,380 --> 00:40:37,120
Once the divers attach cables to the logs, workers on vessels hoist them up.

478
00:40:37,121 --> 00:40:44,120
The species recovered from the Great Lakes include pine, birch, oak, and hemlock.

479
00:40:44,121 --> 00:40:54,120
Half a world away in New Zealand, there's an even rarer treasure than the logs of the Great Lakes.

480
00:40:54,121 --> 00:40:57,120
It's called kauri.

481
00:40:57,121 --> 00:41:04,120
These trees fell over up to 50,000 years ago, were submersed in water,

482
00:41:04,121 --> 00:41:09,360
in water, which eventually became a bog, which eventually became peat.

483
00:41:10,140 --> 00:41:16,600
As the peat formed a shield against oxygen, no fungus could attack the wood, and it survived intact.

484
00:41:18,280 --> 00:41:24,460
Because European settlers clear-cut the Kauri forests, only a small amount of living trees remain.

485
00:41:26,240 --> 00:41:30,900
Rare and now protected in preserves, some are up to 2,000 years old.

486
00:41:30,901 --> 00:41:38,020
At the beginning of the 20th century, the ancient fallen kauri were discovered by farmers

487
00:41:38,021 --> 00:41:41,300
when the surrounding ground settled and exposed the logs.

488
00:41:45,580 --> 00:41:49,800
Today, kauri is found the same way, more or less by accident.

489
00:42:09,960 --> 00:42:15,120
When the logs are located, bulldozers and backhoes are brought in to extract them.

490
00:42:15,121 --> 00:42:18,480
The cowrie wood is not only rare, but remarkably beautiful.

491
00:42:20,420 --> 00:42:23,460
And yet, despite its 50,000-year age,

492
00:42:24,040 --> 00:42:25,300
cowrie is easy to work with

493
00:42:25,301 --> 00:42:28,120
and is used to make fine furniture and cabinetry.

494
00:42:29,500 --> 00:42:33,660
The characteristics of ancient cowrie are not too different

495
00:42:33,661 --> 00:42:35,560
from a tree that you'll find today

496
00:42:35,561 --> 00:42:39,080
as far as the qualities of working with the wood itself.

497
00:42:40,360 --> 00:42:42,640
However, that's where the similarities end.

498
00:42:42,641 --> 00:42:49,640
The appearance of the wood is substantially different in that really, essentially, a phenomena

499
00:42:49,641 --> 00:42:55,240
has occurred inside the wood itself which brings the grain of the wood to a shimmer,

500
00:42:55,241 --> 00:43:01,240
to a sheen that's not seen ordinarily in timbers today.

501
00:43:01,241 --> 00:43:05,580
These unique characteristics are a result of tens of thousands of years of exposure to

502
00:43:05,581 --> 00:43:10,660
ground water and minerals in the peat.

503
00:43:10,661 --> 00:43:14,380
Not surprisingly, cowrie is expensive.

504
00:43:14,381 --> 00:43:20,060
It can range from $25 to $100 per board foot, depending on the particular qualities of the

505
00:43:20,061 --> 00:43:23,060
grain.

506
00:43:23,061 --> 00:43:27,880
The growing popularity of ancient cowrie is yet another example of the encompassing nature

507
00:43:27,881 --> 00:43:31,320
of the modern lumberyard.

508
00:43:31,321 --> 00:43:37,860
Over time, new products and new innovations have kept this ancient industry evolving.

509
00:43:37,861 --> 00:43:41,800
Yet fundamentally, the lumberyard itself remains unchanged.

510
00:43:42,580 --> 00:43:45,600
One of the things about lumber is that it's bulky.

511
00:43:45,760 --> 00:43:47,020
It's a physical product.

512
00:43:47,380 --> 00:43:52,200
It's not something that can be UPSed or Amazon.commed very easily.

513
00:43:54,180 --> 00:43:57,540
So we can count on the traditional services of the lumberyard

514
00:43:57,541 --> 00:44:00,000
to prevail for a long time to come.

515
00:44:02,560 --> 00:44:04,540
The Mona Lisa is a painting of a mystery.

516
00:44:05,060 --> 00:44:07,640
We get the elaboration of this myth of secrecy.

517
00:44:07,860 --> 00:44:09,720
But the Mona Lisa's smile.

518
00:44:09,721 --> 00:44:11,300
Who is the Mona Lisa?

519
00:44:11,301 --> 00:44:14,200
Is she Leonardo's lover?

520
00:44:14,201 --> 00:44:16,220
Is she Leonardo himself?

521
00:44:16,221 --> 00:44:19,680
Da Vinci, decoded Sunday night at 9,

522
00:44:19,681 --> 00:44:23,100
as only the History Channel can bring you.

